# Target C-II — near-full-shore exact-force blocks can have growing depth and vanishing normalized complexity

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only high-`t` diagonal strengthening; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** the no-positive-density-TB1 branch of the selected one-sided high-`t` shore through `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8687`  
**Dependencies:** near-full-shore deep wide-force block theorem; coupled host-margin sharpening  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_high_t_near_full_shore_growing_force_diagonal_t18687.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The near-full-shore force-block theorem is stated for every fixed radius and fixed parameter choice. A diagonal choice lets the radius tend to infinity while the retained center density tends to the entire one-sided-shore density

\[
{c\over2}={113\over10{,}000{,}000}=1.13\times10^{-5}.
\]

At the same time the pair angle may tend to zero slowly enough that every force diamond remains much wider than the canonical `O(D^(3/4))` midpoint error, while all normalized local switching/KKT complexity tends to zero.

Put

\[
c={113\over5{,}000{,}000}.
\]

There is a survivor subsequence `D_k->infinity`, integers `r_k->infinity`, and center families `Q_k` such that

\[
\boxed{
|Q_k|>\left({c\over2}-o(1)\right)D_k.
}
\tag{GD.1}

For every center `i in Q_k`, the full radius-`r_k` block is exact-antipodal and all `2r_k+1` internal interfaces carry exact positive two-ray force pairs. More precisely one may arrange simultaneously:

\[
\boxed{r_k\to\infty,\qquad r_k=o(D_k),}
\tag{GD.2}

\[
\boxed{\rho_k\to0,\qquad D_k^{1/4}\rho_k\to\infty,}
\tag{GD.3}

\[
\boxed{{T_{r,k}\over D_k}\to0,\qquad {T_{0,k}\over D_k}\to0,}
\tag{GD.4}

and hence

\[
\boxed{
\max_{j\in[i-r_k,i+r_k]} B_j=o(1),
\qquad
\max_j {|G_j|\over D_k}=o(1).
}
\tag{GD.5}

Every internal interface has a force-pair projective gap at least `rho_k`. Its two force bars are bilateral and, using the coupled host sharpening, each satisfies

\[
\boxed{
|d_j^\pm\cdot Jv_i|\ge c_H-o(1),
\qquad
c_H=0.453819885927\ldots .
}
\tag{GD.6}

Thus every elementary exact-antipodal dipole in the growing block remains uniformly first-order jammed:

\[
\boxed{
|\ell_{h_j^\pm}(s d^{(j)})|
\ge(0.907639771854\ldots-o(1))D_k|s|.
}
\tag{GD.7}

The force-diamond central radius satisfies

\[
{D_k\over2}\sin{\rho_k\over2}-L_{D_k}
\sim {D_k\rho_k\over4},
\tag{GD.8}

and by (GD.3)

\[
\boxed{{D_k\rho_k\over L_{D_k}}\to\infty.}
\tag{GD.9}

So the force diamonds remain geometrically resolved on a scale asymptotically much larger than the midpoint uncertainty even though their normalized opening angle tends to zero.

Finally all rows in the block have one exact oriented phase, all reactions point in one ordinary direction, and their magnitudes telescope:

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{j=i-r_k}^{i+r_k}|G_j|<D_k.
}
\tag{GD.10}

Consequently at least half of the internal interfaces satisfy

\[
\boxed{
|G_j|<{D_k\over r_k}+o(D_k/r_k),
}
\tag{GD.11}

while still carrying the uniformly transverse bilateral force pair (GD.6)--(GD.7).

---

## 1. A concrete fixed-`n` parameter schedule

For integer `n>=1`, set

\[
r_n=n,
\qquad
m_n=2n+1,
\tag{GD.12}
\]

\[
\varepsilon_n={c\over10n},
\qquad
\eta_{0,n}=\eta_{r,n}={\varepsilon_n\over4}={c\over40n},
\tag{GD.13}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
\rho_n={\varepsilon_n\over8m_n}
={c\over80n(2n+1)}.
}
\tag{GD.14}

Then

\[
2m_n\rho_n={\varepsilon_n\over4}={c\over40n}.
\tag{GD.15}

Choose fixed thresholds for this `n`, for example

\[
T_{0,n}=41n,
\qquad
T_{r,n}=41m_nn.
\tag{GD.16}

They obey

\[
{c\over T_{0,n}}<{c\over40n}=\eta_{0,n},
\]

and

\[
{m_nc\over T_{r,n}}={c\over41n}<{c\over40n}=\eta_{r,n}.
\]

The near-full-shore theorem therefore gives, for all sufficiently large `D` at this fixed `n`, center density

\[
\begin{aligned}
\Lambda_n
&={c\over2}-\eta_{0,n}-\eta_{r,n}-2m_n\rho_n\\
&={c\over2}-{3c\over40n}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{GD.17}

Hence

\[
\boxed{\Lambda_n\uparrow c/2.}
\tag{GD.18}

Every retained radius-`n` neighborhood is exact phase and `T_{r,n}`-good throughout, every internal interface has pair gap at least `rho_n`, and the center is `T_{0,n}`-good.

---

## 2. Diagonalize while dominating every finite-source error

For each `n`, the fixed-parameter theorem holds beyond some finite source threshold `D_*(n)`. Choose a strictly increasing sequence `D_n` so large that, in addition to `D_n>=D_*(n)`, all of the following hold:

\[
{n\over D_n}< {1\over n},
\qquad
{T_{r,n}\over D_n}< {1\over n},
\qquad
{T_{0,n}\over D_n}< {1\over n},
\tag{GD.19}

\[
D_n^{1/4}\rho_n>n,
\tag{GD.20}

and every `o(D)` or `o(1)` remainder in the fixed-`n` theorem is smaller than `D_n/n` or `1/n` respectively.

Apply the fixed theorem at source `D_n` and put

\[
r_k=n,\quad \rho_k=\rho_n,\quad T_{r,k}=T_{r,n},\quad T_{0,k}=T_{0,n}.
\]

Equations (GD.2)--(GD.5) follow immediately from (GD.14), (GD.16), and (GD.19)--(GD.20). The density estimate is

\[
|Q_k|>
\left({c\over2}-{3c\over40k}-o(1)\right)D_k,
\]

which is (GD.1).

This is an ordinary diagonal extraction; no uniform-in-`r` asymptotic statement is assumed.

---

## 3. The pair gap shrinks, but the force diamonds remain resolved

The canonical midpoint error has size

\[
L_D=O(D^{3/4}).
\]

For small `rho`,

\[
\sin(\rho/2)\sim\rho/2.
\]

The common central disk inside each force diamond has radius

\[
R_{\rm dia}(D,\rho)
={D\over2}\sin{\rho\over2}-L_D.
\tag{GD.21}

Condition (GD.20) says

\[
D_k^{1/4}\rho_k\to\infty.
\]

Therefore

\[
D_k\rho_k\gg D_k^{3/4}\asymp L_{D_k},
\]

which proves (GD.8)--(GD.9). In particular the four endpoints of every selected pair remain distinct and the two diameter bars cross centrally for all sufficiently large `k`.

The pair angle is allowed to shrink only because the host-derived distance of each individual bar from the reaction axis stays fixed at `gamma_H-o(1)`.

---

## 4. Normalized complexity and KKT scale vanish throughout the block

At every internal interface the fixed theorem gives

\[
B_j<{T_{r,k}C\over D_k},
\qquad
0<|G_j|<T_{r,k}C.
\]

Since `T_{r,k}/D_k->0`, this proves (GD.5) after absorbing the fixed constant `C` into the diagonal choice in (GD.19).

The exact force coefficients obey

\[
(c_H-o(1)){|G_j|\over D_k}
\le\lambda_j^\pm
\le {T_{r,k}C\over D_k\sin\rho_k}.
\tag{GD.22}

The diagonal may, if desired, be chosen still larger so that

\[
{T_{r,k}\over D_k\rho_k}\to0.
\tag{GD.23}

Then every selected compressed force coefficient itself tends to zero, while the geometric first-order cusp (GD.7) remains macroscopic after normalization by `D_k`.

This separates the two notions cleanly: KKT mass can vanish absolutely while every local dipole direction remains uniformly jammed.

---

## 5. The ordered reaction stack contains many especially small steps

On each exact-phase block, the near-full-shore theorem gives

\[
F_j=A_jv_i,
\]

with the `A_j` strictly monotone and

\[
\sum_{j=i-r_k}^{i+r_k}|G_j|
=|A_{i-r_k}-A_{i+r_k+1}|<D_k.
\]

There are `m_k=2r_k+1` positive terms. Therefore at least half satisfy

\[
|G_j|<{2D_k\over m_k}
<{D_k\over r_k}+o(D_k/r_k),
\]

proving (GD.11).

Those interfaces still carry bilateral force pairs with pair gap at least `rho_k`, fixed host transverse margin `c_H-o(1)`, and the same exact row phase as the entire block.

---

## 6. Frontier effect

On the no-strong-TB1 side, essentially the **entire one-sided shore density** can now be represented by centers of growing exact-force plateaux:

```text
(c/2-o(1))D centers;
r_k -> infinity exact-antipodal interfaces on either side of every center;
every internal interface carries an exact positive force pair;
pair gap rho_k -> 0 but D^(1/4) rho_k -> infinity;
every force ray stays >=26.989 degrees from the reaction axis;
normalized local switching/KKT complexity -> 0;
all reactions are collinear, nonzero, and strictly ordered;
more than half of the reactions in each block are O(D/r_k).
```

This is stronger than a fixed-depth or fixed-density diagonal plateau. It rules out an escape in which the exact-force structure becomes sparse as the exact-phase depth grows.

C-II remains open. The unresolved issue is now highly concentrated: one must show that a growing collinear reaction stack with vanishing normalized KKT scale, but uniformly transverse centrally crossing diameter force pairs at every interface, is incompatible with the exact normal order/common train geometry, or else use it to produce a strict diameter descent.

**Disposition:** diagonalized the near-full-shore fixed-radius theorem to growing depth while recovering asymptotically the full one-sided-shore density and preserving geometrically resolved, uniformly transverse exact force pairs at every internal interface.